# follow-up 2): a conversation switch at --parallel 1 copies
# out, and allocates, only the kept states' rows the incoming request can write, not their whole states.
# Before: the first request of another conversation saved every kept state still held in the live caches whole: with
# TF_GLM_ROW_LINEAGE the first write copied each one's rows from its start to the end of the caches (rows_written ->
# _flush -> _fill) into a whole copy save_rows allocated (torch.empty_like); without it _take_over -> save_rows cloned
# them. Both linear in the kept tokens: laptop rig, a cold 49-token prompt after a warm turn of a 200k / 300k
# conversation +20..+35 / +52..+83 ms TTFT (GB10 at ~205k: +58-72 ms, P1ShortTTFT).
# Now (TF_GLM_SAVE_BOUND, default on, both lineage modes):
# - a write declares its end: rows_written(st, start, end); _run_once passes decode.write_end (the prompt, and with a
#   reply past its first token prompt + max_tokens: the last row a verify window can reach, since every drafter caps
#   its depth / copy room at max_tokens - len(out)); a shared-prefix / grid stop's prefill passes the stop;
# - a saved state's rows stay pending in the caches as ranges (snap._pend: token and pooled-key row ranges); a write
#   copies out only the pending rows in [start, end) and the pools it completes [start // 4, end // 4)
#   (sparse._pool_keys stores complete pools only); rows past the end keep their old write's lineage tag;
# - the copies are kept as pieces of the rows copied (decode._Pieces): nothing is allocated at the switch (the whole
#   empty_like copy alone cost as much as the copy: a new segment of the kept tokens' size); complete_rows makes them
#   whole before the spill tier reads them (engine._spill_snap); nbytes, and every keep / drop decision, unchanged;
#   _fill is all or nothing (1b): every plane's pieces are made first (_Pieces.take returns a piece, adds nothing)
#   and added, with the pending rows taken off and the counters moved, only once all exist, so an allocation failure
#   leaves the copy and _pend as they were and the retry copies each row once (SwitchReview LOW-1);
# - a forward past the declared end (forward.stage) saves the rows it reaches first (decode.rows_past): a short end
#   costs a late copy, never a row; a write with no end (prefill(rows_end=None): callers other than _run_once) saves
#   every row from its start, as before;
# - load_rows copies back only rows it copied out (pending ones are intact) and, with the lineage, only where the
#   lineages differ (as ranges, _unlike), plus the pool row past the state's end; the tokens of that pool past n get a
#   new tag, rows past it keep theirs.
# TF_GLM_ROW_LINEAGE=0 with the bound: the same pending rows and pieces, no lineage (a load copies back every row it
# copied out). TF_GLM_SAVE_BOUND=0: as before (lineage off: every row out and back at once; on: every write runs to
# the end, into a whole copy). Rank-local: every rank computes the same end from max_tokens (follow_one's header) and
# saves its own share. After: +1 to +3 ms at 50k-300k. Replies, live caches and every kept state's reload
# bit-identical to the tree without this patch (tests below).
